## 1.  What is the Repository Pattern?

| **Repository** | **DAO** |
|----------------|---------|
| *Domain‑centric*: the interface lives in the domain layer and looks like a collection (`get(id)`, `findAll()`, …). | *Persistence‑centric*: the interface is tied to the persistence API (`EntityManager`, `Session`, `Connection`). |
| Hides the persistence mechanism – the domain code never sees JDBC, JPA, Hibernate, etc. | Exposes the persistence API to the domain layer. |
| Enables clean architecture / hexagonal architecture – the domain talks to the repository, the infrastructure implements it. | Couples the domain to a particular persistence library. |

> **Bottom line:**  
> The Repository pattern gives you a *clean, testable, domain‑first* API for persisting domain objects.  
> In Java the pattern is usually expressed as an *interface* (in the domain) plus one or more *implementations* (in the infrastructure).

---

## 2.  Typical Repository Interface

```java
// Domain – sits in the domain layer
public interface Repository<T, ID> {

    /** Find an entity by its id. */
    Optional<T> findById(ID id);

    /** Return all entities. */
    List<T> findAll();

    /** Persist a new or updated entity. */
    T save(T entity);

    /** Delete an entity. */
    void delete(T entity);
}
```

> *Why `Optional`?*  
> `Optional` makes the “not found” case explicit and forces the caller to handle it.

---

## 3.  Domain‑Specific Repository

```java
// Domain – domain‑specific
public interface UserRepository extends Repository<User, Long> {
    Optional<User> findByUsername(String username);
}
```

> Keep **only** domain‑level operations in the interface.  
> Any query that is “domain‑specific” should live here; the rest is covered by the generic `Repository`.

---

## 4.  Implementations

### 4.1  Manual JPA Implementation (no Spring Data)

```java
package com.example.infrastructure.repository.jpa;

import com.example.domain.User;
import com.example.domain.UserRepository;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import jakarta.persistence.PersistenceContext;
import jakarta.transaction.Transactional;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Repository;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

@Repository
@Transactional // optional – you can put it on the Service instead
public class JpaUserRepository implements UserRepository {

    @PersistenceContext
    private EntityManager em;

    @Override
    public Optional<User> findById(Long id) {
        return Optional.ofNullable(em.find(User.class, id));
    }

    @Override
    public List<User> findAll() {
        return em.createQuery("SELECT u FROM User u", User.class)
                 .getResultList();
    }

    @Override
    public User save(User entity) {
        if (entity.getId() == null) {
            em.persist(entity);
            return entity;
        } else {
            return em.merge(entity);
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void delete(User entity) {
        em.remove(em.contains(entity) ? entity : em.merge(entity));
    }

    @Override
    public Optional<User> findByUsername(String username) {
        return em.createQuery("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.username = :uname", User.class)
                 .setParameter("uname", username)
                 .getResultStream()
                 .findFirst();
    }
}
```

> *Key points*  
> * `EntityManager` is injected via `@PersistenceContext`.  
> * `@Repository` is optional but gives Spring exception translation.  
> * `@Transactional` can be placed here or in the Service layer – it depends on your transaction strategy.

### 4.2  Using Spring Data JPA (automatic implementation)

```java
package com.example.infrastructure.repository.jpa;

import com.example.domain.User;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Repository;

import java.util.Optional;

@Repository
public interface SpringDataUserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    Optional<User> findByUsername(String username);
}
```

> **No code required** – Spring Data will generate a class at runtime that implements the interface.  
> If you want to keep the domain repository (`UserRepository`) separate, you can simply `@Autowired` the Spring Data interface in a thin wrapper that implements `UserRepository`.

```java
@Component
public class UserRepositoryAdapter implements UserRepository {

    private final SpringDataUserRepository springRepo;

    public UserRepositoryAdapter(SpringDataUserRepository springRepo) {
        this.springRepo = springRepo;
    }

    @Override public Optional<User> findById(Long id)  { return springRepo.findById(id); }
    @Override public List<User> findAll()             { return springRepo.findAll(); }
    @Override public User save(User entity)           { return springRepo.save(entity); }
    @Override public void delete(User entity)         { springRepo.delete(entity); }
    @Override public Optional<User> findByUsername(String username) {
        return springRepo.findByUsername(username);
    }
}
```

