## 5.  Using a Repository in the Service Layer

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor // Lombok for constructor injection
@Transactional // Optional: can be placed on each method instead
public class UserService {

    private final UserRepository userRepository;

    public User register(UserDto dto) {
        // Domain validation …
        User user = new User(dto.getUsername(), dto.getEmail(), /* … */);
        return userRepository.save(user);
    }

    public User getById(Long id) {
        return userRepository.findById(id)
                             .orElseThrow(() -> new UserNotFoundException(id));
    }

    public List<User> listAll() {
        return userRepository.findAll();
    }

    public void delete(Long id) {
        User user = getById(id);
        userRepository.delete(user);
    }
}
```

> *Why not call `EntityManager` directly in the Service?*  
> You keep the domain layer independent of persistence technology and you can swap the implementation (e.g., switch from JPA to MongoDB) without touching business logic.

---

## 6.  Unit Testing a Repository

### 6.1  Testing the Domain‑Level Interface (Mocked Implementation)

```java
class UserServiceTest {

    private UserRepository userRepository = mock(UserRepository.class);
    private UserService userService = new UserService(userRepository);

    @Test
    void shouldRegisterUser() {
        UserDto dto = new UserDto("alice", "alice@example.com");
        User saved = new User(1L, "alice", "alice@example.com");
        when(userRepository.save(any(User.class))).thenReturn(saved);

        User result = userService.register(dto);

        assertEquals(saved, result);
        verify(userRepository).save(any(User.class));
    }
}
```

### 6.2  Integration Test with an In‑Memory DB (Spring Boot)

```java
@DataJpaTest
class JpaUserRepositoryTest {

    @Autowired
    private JpaUserRepository repo;

    @Test
    void findByUsernameWorks() {
        User user = new User(null, "bob", "bob@example.com");
        repo.save(user);

        Optional<User> found = repo.findByUsername("bob");

        assertTrue(found.isPresent());
        assertEquals("bob", found.get().getUsername());
    }
}
```

> `@DataJpaTest` starts an H2 (or other) in‑memory DB and configures Spring Data.  
> Use `@Transactional` on the test to roll back after each test.

